---
import Layout from "../../../layouts/Layout.astro";

const question = "What are the building regulations for dry lining in Maidstone?";
const answer = "Building Regulations are a set of national standards, set by the Secretary of State, that apply to most building work in England (separate but equivalent regimes operate in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland). In Maidstone the Building Control function is delivered by Maidstone Borough Council Building Control — for domestic work — or by an approved inspector (private Building Control) if you choose that route. The regulations themselves do not change between authorities.\n\nThe Approved Documents that most often govern drylining work in Maidstone:\n\n- Part A (Structure): applies if you are removing or modifying a load-bearing wall, or if your work alters the structural stability of the building\n- Part B (Fire Safety): Volume 1 (dwellings) and Volume 2 (buildings other than dwellings) set the requirements for fire-rated plasterboard on escape routes, garage ceilings and walls separating a garage from living space, and compartmentation in flats, HMOs and commercial buildings. Fire resistance periods are typically expressed in minutes and tested to BS 476 (or the European equivalent BS EN 1364 series)\n- Part C (Dampness): moisture-resistant plasterboard in wet areas and a vapour-control layer on the warm side of insulation where condensation could otherwise occur\n- Part E (Resistance to Sound): sets acoustic performance targets for separating walls and floors between flats (typically 45 dB Rw for new-build conversions), between rooms in residential conversions, and between bedsits in HMOs (typically 40 dB Rw). Performance is measured to BS EN ISO 717\n- Part F (Ventilation): sets minimum extract rates for kitchens, bathrooms and utility rooms. Background ventilation (trickle vents or a PIV unit) is required in refurbished homes\n- Part L (Conservation of fuel and power): sets target U-values for the thermal envelope. For solid-wall retrofit, current guidance targets a U-value of 0.30 W/m²K or better for the upgraded wall — confirmed by an insulation layer (typically 50–80mm PIR-backed insulated plasterboard) and a continuous vapour control layer\n- Part M (Access to and use of buildings): relevant if you are working in a building that must comply with access requirements (e.g. HMO common parts, commercial buildings open to the public) — sets standards for doors, thresholds, and WCs\n\nCurrent fee schedule and inspection booking windows: [VERIFY against current MBC Building Control fee schedule — fees change annually, MBC's published scale is the only authoritative source]. Typical inspection stages are pre-plasterboard (cavities and services visible) and completion. Booking lead times vary; for non-urgent work, allow around a week.\n\nHow we work on Maidstone jobs that need Building Control:\n\n1. We file the Building Control application (or your approved inspector) at the design stage, not after work starts\n2. We schedule the pre-plasterboard inspection before any boards go up so the inspector can see insulation, services, fire-stopping and acoustic insulation in the cavity\n3. We arrange the completion inspection and pass you the certificate\n\nFor non-notifiable work (e.g. like-for-like replacement plasterboard in a domestic room with no change to fire, acoustic or thermal performance) we still build to BS 8212 best practice but no formal sign-off is required.";
const description = "Building Regulations are a set of national standards, set by the Secretary of State, that apply to most building work in England (separate but equivalent";
const canonical = "/drylining-faq/what-are-the-building-regulations-for-dry-lining-in-maidstone/";
const has_pricing = true;

const schema = {"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What are the building regulations for dry lining in Maidstone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Building Regulations are a set of national standards, set by the Secretary of State, that apply to most building work in England (separate but equivalent regimes operate in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland). In Maidstone the Building Control function is delivered by Maidstone Borough Council Building Control — for domestic work — or by an approved inspector (private Building Control) if you choose that route. The regulations themselves do not change between authorities.  The Approved Documents that most often govern drylining work in Maidstone:  - Part A (Structure): applies if you are removing or modifying a load-bearing wall, or if your work alters the structural stability of the building - Part B (Fire Safety): Volume 1 (dwellings) and Volume 2 (buildings other than dwellings) set the requirements for fire-rated plasterboard on escape routes, garage ceilings and walls separating a garage from living space, and compartmentation in flats, HMOs and commercial buildings. Fire resistance periods are typically expressed in minutes and tested to BS 476 (or the European equivalent BS EN 1364 series) - Part C (Dampness): moisture-resistant plasterboard in wet areas and a vapour-control layer on the warm side of insulation where condensation could otherwise occur - Part E (Resistance to Sound): sets acoustic performance targets for separating walls and floors between flats (typically 45 dB Rw for new-build conversions), between rooms in residential conversions, and between bedsits in HMOs (typically 40 dB Rw). Performance is measured to BS EN ISO 717 - Part F (Ventilation): sets minimum extract rates for kitchens, bathrooms and utility rooms. Background ventilation (trickle vents or a PIV unit) is required in refurbished homes - Part L (Conservation of fuel and power): sets target U-values for the thermal envelope. For solid-wall retrofit, current guidance targets a U-value of 0.30 W/m²K or better for the upgraded wall — confirmed by an insulation layer (typically 50–80mm PIR-backed insulated plasterboard) and a continuous vapour control layer - Part M (Access to and use of buildings): relevant if you are working in a building that must comply with access requirements (e.g. HMO common parts, commercial buildings open to the public) — sets standards for doors, thresholds, and WCs  Current fee schedule and inspection booking windows: [VERIFY against current MBC Building Control fee schedule — fees change annually, MBC's published scale is the only authoritative source]. Typical inspection stages are pre-plasterboard (cavities and services visible) and completion. Booking lead times vary; for non-urgent work, allow around a week.  How we work on Maidstone jobs that need Building Control:  1. We file the Building Control application (or your approved inspector) at the design stage, not after work starts 2. We schedule the pre-plasterboard inspection before any boards go up so the inspector can see insulation, services, fire-stopping and acoustic insulation in the cavity 3. We arrange the completion inspection and pass you the certificate  For non-notifiable work (e.g. like-for-like replacement plasterboard in a domestic room with no change to fire, acoustic or thermal performance) we still build to BS 8212 best practice but no formal sign-off is required."}}]},{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://dryliningmaidstone.co.uk/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"FAQ","item":"https://dryliningmaidstone.co.uk/faq/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"What are the building regulations for dry lining in Maidstone?","item":"https://dryliningmaidstone.co.uk/drylining-faq/what-are-the-building-regulations-for-dry-lining-in-maidstone/"}]}]};
---

<Layout
  title={`${question} | Drylining Maidstone`}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  schema={schema}
>
  <!-- HERO — matches new site design: dark navy gradient + yellow accent + breadcrumb + badge + H1 + CTAs -->
  <section style="position:relative;min-height:380px;display:flex;align-items:center;background:linear-gradient(135deg,#0a0e1a 0%,#1a1f3a 100%);overflow:hidden;">
    <img src="/images/maidstone-town.jpg" alt="Maidstone town centre — backdrop for the Drylining Maidstone FAQ" style="position:absolute;inset:0;width:100%;height:100%;object-fit:cover;opacity:0.20;" />
    <div style="position:absolute;inset:0;background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(10,14,26,0.88) 0%,rgba(26,31,58,0.88) 100%);"></div>
    <div class="container" style="position:relative;z-index:1;">
      <nav style="font-size:0.75rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.6);margin-bottom:1.25rem;">
        <a href="/" style="color:#FACC15;">HOME</a> / <a href="/faq/" style="color:#FACC15;">FAQ</a> / <span style="color:#FACC15;font-weight:600;">{question.slice(0, 60)}{question.length > 60 ? "…" : ""}</span>
      </nav>
      <span style="display:inline-block;padding:0.375rem 1rem;background:rgba(30,64,175,0.15);border:1px solid #FACC15;color:#FACC15;font-size:0.75rem;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.05em;border-radius:99px;margin-bottom:1.25rem;">MAIDSTONE DRYLINING FAQ</span>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(1.75rem,4vw,3rem);font-weight:800;color:var(--white);line-height:1.15;margin-bottom:1rem;max-width:48rem;">{question}</h1>
      <p style="font-size:1.0625rem;color:rgba(255,255,255,0.85);margin-bottom:1.5rem;max-width:42rem;">Maidstone-specific answer with current 2026 pricing, building regulations, HMO licensing rules and practical advice from our local team.</p>
      <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;">
        <a href="/get-quote/" class="btn btn-highlight">Get My Free Quote</a>
        <a href="/faq/" class="btn btn-outline" style="color:var(--white);border-color:rgba(255,255,255,0.3);">All FAQs</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="container" style="padding:3rem 1.5rem 4rem;">
    <article class="card" style="padding:2rem;max-width:880px;">
      <div style="background:rgba(30,64,175,0.05);border-left:4px solid #FACC15;padding:1rem 1.5rem;margin-bottom:1.75rem;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
        <p style="margin:0;font-size:0.875rem;color:var(--text);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em;font-weight:700;">Quick Answer</p>
        <p style="margin:0.5rem 0 0;font-size:1.0625rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.7;font-weight:500;">{description}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-answer" style="font-size:1.0625rem;line-height:1.85;color:var(--text);">
  <p style="margin:0 0 1.1rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;">Building Regulations are a set of national standards, set by the Secretary of State, that apply to most building work in England (separate but equivalent regimes operate in Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland). In Maidstone the Building Control function is delivered by Maidstone Borough Council Building Control — for domestic work — or by an approved inspector (private Building Control) if you choose that route. The regulations themselves do not change between authorities.</p>
  <p style="margin:0 0 1.1rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;">The Approved Documents that most often govern drylining work in Maidstone:</p>
  <ul style="list-style:disc;padding-left:1.5rem;margin:0 0 1.25rem;color:var(--text);">
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part A (Structure): applies if you are removing or modifying a load-bearing wall, or if your work alters the structural stability of the building</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part B (Fire Safety): Volume 1 (dwellings) and Volume 2 (buildings other than dwellings) set the requirements for fire-rated plasterboard on escape routes, garage ceilings and walls separating a garage from living space, and compartmentation in flats, HMOs and commercial buildings. Fire resistance periods are typically expressed in minutes and tested to BS 476 (or the European equivalent BS EN 1364 series)</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part C (Dampness): moisture-resistant plasterboard in wet areas and a vapour-control layer on the warm side of insulation where condensation could otherwise occur</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part E (Resistance to Sound): sets acoustic performance targets for separating walls and floors between flats (typically 45 dB Rw for new-build conversions), between rooms in residential conversions, and between bedsits in HMOs (typically 40 dB Rw). Performance is measured to BS EN ISO 717</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part F (Ventilation): sets minimum extract rates for kitchens, bathrooms and utility rooms. Background ventilation (trickle vents or a PIV unit) is required in refurbished homes</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part L (Conservation of fuel and power): sets target U-values for the thermal envelope. For solid-wall retrofit, current guidance targets a U-value of 0.30 W/m²K or better for the upgraded wall — confirmed by an insulation layer (typically 50–80mm PIR-backed insulated plasterboard) and a continuous vapour control layer</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">Part M (Access to and use of buildings): relevant if you are working in a building that must comply with access requirements (e.g. HMO common parts, commercial buildings open to the public) — sets standards for doors, thresholds, and WCs</li>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin:0 0 1.1rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;">Current fee schedule and inspection booking windows: [VERIFY against current MBC Building Control fee schedule — fees change annually, MBC's published scale is the only authoritative source]. Typical inspection stages are pre-plasterboard (cavities and services visible) and completion. Booking lead times vary; for non-urgent work, allow around a week.</p>
  <p style="margin:0 0 1.1rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;">How we work on Maidstone jobs that need Building Control:</p>
  <ol style="list-style:decimal;padding-left:1.5rem;margin:0 0 1.25rem;color:var(--text);">
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">We file the Building Control application (or your approved inspector) at the design stage, not after work starts</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">We schedule the pre-plasterboard inspection before any boards go up so the inspector can see insulation, services, fire-stopping and acoustic insulation in the cavity</li>
    <li style="margin-bottom:0.45rem;line-height:1.75;">We arrange the completion inspection and pass you the certificate</li>
  </ol>
  <p style="margin:0 0 1.1rem;color:var(--text);line-height:1.8;">For non-notifiable work (e.g. like-for-like replacement plasterboard in a domestic room with no change to fire, acoustic or thermal performance) we still build to BS 8212 best practice but no formal sign-off is required.</p>
      </div>

      {has_pricing && (
        <div style="margin-top:2rem;padding:1.25rem 1.5rem;background:#fef3c7;border-left:4px solid #f59e0b;border-radius:0 8px 8px 0;">
          <p style="margin:0;font-size:0.875rem;font-weight:700;color:#92400e;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:0.05em;">Pricing & figures — read this</p>
          <p style="margin:0.5rem 0 0;font-size:0.9375rem;color:#78350f;line-height:1.7;">Any prices, fees or figure-based guidance on this page are indicative and intended for budget planning only. They are not a quote and not a substitute for the published source. For current fees always check: <strong>Maidstone Borough Council Building Control fee schedule</strong>, <strong>Maidstone Borough Council HMO licensing fees</strong>, <strong>BCIS construction cost indices</strong>, and the <strong>current Approved Documents</strong> (free PDFs from GOV.UK). Send us your plans and we will turn around a fixed-price written quote within 48 hours.</p>
        </div>
      )}

      <div style="margin-top:2.5rem;padding:1.5rem;background:var(--accent);border-radius:var(--radius);">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.25rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--secondary);margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Need a quote for this in Maidstone?</h3>
        <p style="color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1.25rem;font-size:1rem;">Send a brief, your plans or just a few photos. We will turn around a fixed-price quote within 48 hours, anywhere across ME14–ME20, TN12 and TN27 postcodes.</p>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:0.75rem;flex-wrap:wrap;">
          <a href="/get-quote/" class="btn btn-primary">Get My Free Quote</a>
          <a href="/contact/" class="btn btn-outline">Contact Us</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <aside style="max-width:880px;margin-top:2rem;padding:1.5rem;background:var(--white);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius);">
      <h3 style="font-size:1.125rem;font-weight:700;color:var(--text);margin-bottom:1rem;">Related Questions</h3>
      <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;margin:0;display:grid;gap:0.625rem;">
            <li><a href="/drylining-faq/what-is-dry-lining-and-how-does-it-work/" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:500;">What is dry lining and how does it work?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/drylining-faq/how-much-does-dry-lining-cost-in-maidstone/" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:500;">How much does dry lining cost in Maidstone?</a></li>
            <li><a href="/drylining-faq/is-dry-lining-cheaper-than-traditional-plastering/" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:500;">Is dry lining cheaper than traditional plastering?</a></li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:1.25rem;font-size:0.9375rem;color:var(--text-light);"><a href="/faq/" style="color:var(--primary);font-weight:600;">Browse all 41 FAQs →</a></p>
    </aside>
  </main>
</Layout>
